{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift / level bias: when the last-30d history is in the high-60s/low-70s and the next month transitions into a selloff, the model stays anchored too high. Apply a downward level adjustment once a downside break occurs (i.e., after the first clear drop day, shift the whole path lower).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon. Days 1–7 are mildly low/close; days ~10–30 are systematically too high. Use an increasing negative correction with lead time (small at 1w, larger at 2–4w).",
    "Missed mean reversion after peaks: following a local spike/peak (e.g., >~72–73 in these samples), the model extrapolates strength instead of reverting. Impose mean reversion: cap upside continuation and pull forecasts back toward the recent 10–20d mean after a peak.",
    "Overshoot on late-horizon rallies: when the model projects a renewed rally in weeks 3–4, actual outcomes often continue drifting down. Damp trend components beyond ~15 trading days (reduce slope/volatility; bias toward flat-to-down).",
    "Underestimation of drawdown magnitude: during selloffs the model predicts a shallow decline while realized prices fall much further (into mid-60s/low-60s). Add a drawdown amplifier once a downtrend is established (e.g., if 5–10d momentum is negative, increase the expected decline rate and lower terminal level)."
  ]
}
